please tell me about the part about  tatooine  okay i'm actually from tatooine the sun  planet in star wars  so that's that's a cool way to introduce  yourself in the u.s like everybody knows  about that wind it's uh actually my city  tattoo in in tunisia in north africa  which is the last city before the desert  and where indeed like george lucas and  star wars crew filmed like multiple  episodes and yeah like actually instead  it started there  [Music]  easter deep has reached the point where  it's expanding into the united states  and you have opened an office right here  in the cic but that's why we're here  today um to kind of welcome instadeep  and karim and the whole company to the  community here and we thought it would  be fun to have a discussion i know that  instadeep is working on  a range of problems  then they include  railway scheduling  virus early warning systems for  different viral variants of coping 19 i  assume and other viruses immunotherapy  research  shipping container packing circuit board  routing and protein design  and what's the common computational  thread that links all those things the  common thread between all of these is uh  artificial intelligence but even like  more narrowly what we call decision  making ai which is really about like  helping like industrial leaders in  different fields from biotech to  logistics and others make  decisions in complex space  using super computers and do it very  very quickly so that's sort of like the  unifying technology we're passionate  about ai passionate not only about the  research side we very like open source  we do a lot of public research but also  about the applications in multiple  fields with our partners anybody who's  seen that wonderful documentary about  alphago  kind of has an understanding of how they  taught alphago to be so good at go you  can teach the computer the complete set  of rules to go but there is no complete  set of rules about how to optimally load  a cargo container ship or how to route  all the trains in germany so that they  are exactly on time and at maximum  capacity all the time how how do  problems from industry how can you even  address them  when you don't know the rules alpha zero  in its original formulation uh is a  a two-player like zero-sum game so it's  really like somebody wins somebody loses  and the system effectively plays against  itself and as a consequence uh starts to  learn you know from what works and what  doesn't and my insight at the time which  led to our first actual research paper  is the fact that it plays against itself  so it could do completely crazy random  things but  one will lead to a good outcome and one  will lead to a bad outcome and so if you  improve the probability of what works  and reduce the probability of what  doesn't conceptually you can make  progress the idea we had that in study  by the time was  we're gonna actually create a system  where the algorithm proposes suggestions  and we're going to compare it versus  like their other suggestions uh think  about it like you know you're a very bad  tennis player for example okay  let me tell you how ai will look at it  then so essentially like it doesn't  matter if you're getting like like a  perfect serve or something provided  you're doing it slightly better than  what you are doing so it's the notion of  relative rewards and relative ranking  rather than absolute  that was on paper ranked rewards that  she which she published it was our first  paper at instead  and and remarkably because you know we  were very inspired by our colleagues at  at google and deepmind  david silver himself  uh met with us and read the paper  and he said wow this is amazing  we  essentially  landed  in the world of  like top ai researchers with real  credibility we were a young startup  mainly based in africa with you know uh  like versioning teams in in paris and  london but still small teams and this  convinced me that  the approach we had at instead about  like doing the deep tech innovation  between the developing world and the  developer world  could actually  be productive  and different from what has been done  before and we've just accelerated since  then  that's the point where a lot of startups  probably would open an office in  palo alto or mountain view right or san  francisco or maybe cambridge um  you didn't you kept growing in africa  and also in paris and london i realized  that you know  like the us is a leader in ai it's a  very competitive market it's like winner  takes all type of market it's also a  market where it's relatively difficult  to acquire top talent because your  direct competition is basically are the  tech giants  very well funded startups we didn't have  like significant funding yet  and at the same time it was very clear  to me that we could really grow and  continue to grow organically or teams  in africa with my background in france  it was relatively easy to get known and  meet top talents in france same thing in  the uk so that was the notion and really  like waiting for the company to get to a  level of maturity  and visibility that would make an  expansion in the us  worthwhile is not just to come to the  u.s is to come to us to have a huge  impact to do things that others others  have not done insadep aims to become a  leading decision-making ai company it is  already a leader in europe middle east  and africa and the goal now is to expand  to the u.s and ultimately build a global  company we love to partner with experts  in their field whether it's  like deutsche ban who's a leader in  railway management systems or biontech  who's a leader in biotechnology and  you know the way insta deep operates is  we're very collaborative  and hopefully in future and in  particular in the us we will collaborate  with leaders in their field from whether  it's like food delivery or  airlines or like shipping industry  cambridge and uh like the ecosystem here  from mit to harvard  is uh is world-class and when it comes  to research and we're quite excited  about that we're looking forward just to  build  like really productive research  collaborations  and by having a presence here  developing our partnerships also with  the tech giants uh were partners with  google we partners with nvidia when we  started everybody said you guys are  crazy it will never work do you think  you can do like  high-grade ai research and development  you're fooling yourself well we like we  proved uh the naysayers wrong  and as a consequence indeed i've seen  many young founders of startups in  africa coming to say  thank you because you've shown that this  is possible you've shown that you can  build a globally competitive company  with a bridge between the developing  world and the developed world it's an  exciting moment welcome to cambridge  thank you and may the force be with you  [Music]

